meta {
  name: Update a game
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}{{apiPrefix}}/me/games/{{gameId}}
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "rating": "Perfect"
  }
}

docs {
  True PATCH semantics: the service reads `model_fields_set`, so an omitted field
  means "leave unchanged" and never "reset". Unknown keys are a 422 rather than a
  silent no-op, which is what makes that distinction safe.

  Two editable fields today, `rating` and `system`, both per-user. `rating: ""` or
  `null` clears back to unrated; `system` has no cleared state, so a blank value is
  a 422 and `null` is rejected explicitly (omit the key instead).

  Note what is absent: name, genres, cover and release date live on the SHARED
  catalog row, so editing one would change the game for everyone who owns it. That
  question is still open.

  404 covers both a nonexistent id and someone else's game: the caller's library is
  the whole namespace, so the API never confirms that a row it will not show you
  exists.

  - 200 the updated game
  - 404 no such game in the caller's library
  - 422 unknown rating, blank system, unknown key
  - 429 over the write budget
}
